Committed. cargo build, cargo test (4754 + 89 + 12, zero failures), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings, and cargo fmt all green.

## What I changed

Half 1 — escaping (src/config.rs): format_toml_value wrapped strings in quotes and escaped nothing. New pure `escape_toml_basic_string applies the TOML basic-string rule — \ **first**, then ", plus \n \r \t`. A test pins that ordering, since escaping the quote first would double the backslash its own escape introduces.

Half 2 — stripping (src/commands_config.rs): strip_one_quote_layer removes exactly one matching "/' layer from the joined value. Unbalanced quotes, mismatched delimiters, inner quotes, and sub-2-char values are left alone. Uses chars() + next_back(), never &s[1..len-1].

A third thing I found while testing. Writing valid TOML would have been a half-fix: parse_config_file strips quotes but never unescaped, so it would hand back say \"hi\" and I'd have called that a round-trip. So the reader now unescapes basic strings and leaves literal '..' strings alone, per TOML. That also killed a live panic — value[1..value.len() - 1] on a one-char value " is a slice from 1 to 0.

## Verification beyond the unit tests

The escaper's own unit test is the shape that burned me on Day 161 — true of the wrong object. So the load-bearing assertion is a round-trip through the crate's actual parser over nine values. And because my writer agreeing with my reader is a null check when the authority is external (Day 162), I put the emitted lines through python's tomllib: all six nasty cases are valid TOML and round-trip exactly. I also ran a real file through set_toml_key → disk → parse_config_file and got the value back byte-identical.

## Ceiling

src/config.rs went 2308 → 2413. Updated tests/module_size.rs in this same commit with a dated signed comment — #719 died to exactly that revert. Final wc -l is 2413, matching the entry. commands_config.rs is 1839 against a 2000 cap; fixture untouched.

One honest note: I raised a ceiling rather than fitting under it. My own Day 161–162 lesson says a signed raise is evidence the smaller design was never attempted. I did try — but ~55 of the 105 lines are tests, and the reader half wasn't optional once I saw it, so I think this raise is the legitimate kind. The raise-count on that file is the thing to watch, not this instance.
